## 10.1 Dual-Layer UX

Maintain two levels of presentation:

### Layer A: Everyday User Experience

- Plain Persian wording.
- Familiar financial units.
- Clear and direct controls.
- Short explanations.
- Meaningful defaults.
- Visible uncertainty.
- Simple decision paths.

### Layer B: Professional Analytical Experience

- Methodology.
- Statistical assumptions.
- Model limitations.
- Sample sizes.
- Data provenance.
- Detailed charts.
- Advanced filters.
- Technical comparison tools.

Do not make ordinary users learn specialist terminology to perform basic tasks.

Do not remove advanced functionality from professional users.

## 10.2 Task-Oriented Workspace

Use the following activity model:

- Discover.
- Analyze.
- Evaluate.
- Act.

Evaluate whether existing navigation already supports these tasks.

Preserve independent routes where combining them would harm discoverability or create architectural risk.

Do not blindly create four new top-level tabs.

