- Does this design solve a demonstrated user problem?
- Does it create additional cognitive work?
- Does it conceal useful information?
- Does it introduce a new navigation burden?
- Does it harm experienced users?
- Does it exclude ordinary users?
- Does it create misleading data interpretations?
- Does it make sponsored content appear more credible?
- Does it harm mobile map interaction?
- Does it increase application complexity?
- Does it duplicate existing capabilities?
- Does it weaken security or privacy?
- Does it increase maintenance burden?
- Can the design be tested and reversed?

For every major decision, include the strongest argument against it.

Prefer a simpler solution when it provides equal or better verified outcomes.

Never optimize one screen while damaging the whole product.

Never treat modern appearance as evidence of good usability.

Never treat scientific terminology as proof of design quality.

Never treat an AI-generated design as a validated user experience.

